  "text": "**Solution 1 (p = 2) is not reliable.** Its div-curl step needs one of the two factors to converge strongly, but here both factors are only bounded in L^p and converge weakly. The condition \"1/p + 1/p ≤ 1\" is also not the right pairing condition for this product. The argument never shows why the threshold should be exactly 2, so I don't accept its answer.\n\n**Solution 2 (p = 4/3) is more plausible.** Its mechanism is a standard one. With M = cof D²Φ and m = ∇^⊥ψ, the products that appear are of the form ∇Φ · D²Φ and ψ · M. Using the 2D Sobolev embedding W^{1,p} ↪ L^{p*} with 1/p* = 1/p − 1/2, the strong–weak pairing condition reduces to p ≥ 4/3. This matches the classical weak-continuity threshold for the 2D determinant in W^{1,p}, which I can check by hand: det Du = ∂₁(u₁∂₂u₂) − ∂₂(u₁∂₁u₂), and the Rellich–Kondrachov argument needs u₁ strong in L^{p'}, which requires p > 4/3.\n\nI could not verify either answer against the source paper, since I have no search tool available here. The 4/3 answer rests on a plausible threshold computation, not on a confirmed citation, so my confidence is moderate.\n\nCHOICE: 2",
